== Parents Nurturing, Teaching, and Training Children

=== §. 26. #emph[Of nurturing children well.]

I refer good nurture in part to the temporal good of children, because (as we shall hear later in the particulars) it is a special means of the outward, temporal welfare of the child even in this world. Unless this is added to #emph[nourishing,] in what do rational men and women exceed irrational beasts? The cruelest beasts of all are very tender (as we heard before) toward their young, nourishing them and providing all things needful for them until they can fend for themselves. But as God has given man a rational soul, an understanding head, capacity, teachableness and aptness to learn, so parents ought to make use of those abilities and gifts. Otherwise, for lack of use, they may in time be lost, and so children turn out little better than brute beasts. In this respect the proverb is true, #emph[better unfed than untaught.] Experience shows that a good education is better than a great portion. The Holy Spirit presses this point very much on parents, as we shall hear later in the particulars. For I will handle these three points.

+ The #emph[kinds] of nurture.
+ The #emph[time] when it is to be done.
+ The means of doing it well.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of parents’ neglect in nurturing children.]

Contrary to good nurture is too much liberty, which often brings much woe and utter ruin on children. It is the greatest enemy of good education that can be, and the nurse of all vice. Yet many parents do not mind letting their children live as they please: all the care they take is that they are fed and clothed. This is a common fault of both rich and poor parents. The rich claim that their children need no education, because they have enough to leave them, not knowing that education is a special means to make them keep #emph[that enough] and use it well. The poor claim that they are not able to bring up their children to anything, not considering that the Lord by his providence has so ordered the affairs of men that, as there are fit employments for the greatest, so there are also #footnote[#emph[Si parentum incuria filij in intemperantiam labentur, genitores rei animarum illarum fient. Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 11. I. The kinds of nurture.] for the lowliest, which may be followed without much cost. So the fault lies wholly in the negligence of parents. And if as a result children fall into any wild living, their parents will answer for it.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of parents teaching their children good manners.]

The nurturing of children mentioned above consists

+ In teaching them good manners.
+ In training them up for a good calling.

Not only heathen men and other moralists, who were merely natural, civilized men, but also the Holy Spirit himself has prescribed many rules of good manners, and has urged and pressed them much.

1\. The word #emph[nurture]#footnote[παιδεία. See Treatise 1, §. 119.] mentioned by the apostle in this text which we have in hand implies as much; and so does the phrase which Solomon uses, “Train up a child in the way he should go”#footnote[Prov 22:6.]: that is, teach him how to order the course of his life.

2\. The many #footnote[Lev 19:32. Prov 25:6. Luke 14:8.] precepts about reverencing our superiors and conducting ourselves with respect toward one another are rules of good manners. There are very many of these in Scripture.

3\. Those rules are #footnote[Gen 23:7; 43:33. Job 32:4, 6.] commended by many examples of holy men recorded in Scripture.

4\. Those who have #footnote[Job 30:1, 12. Luke 14:7.] failed in the rules of good manners and behaved rudely are reproved by the Holy Spirit.

Nor is it without good reason that this point is so set forth. For

1\. #emph[Good manners] are a very becoming and seemly thing. But #footnote[1 Cor 14:40.] it befits Christians to do “all things decently.” That #emph[decency] is to be applied not only to the affairs of God’s church but also to the whole course of our life. In this respect we are commanded to “walk #footnote[εὐσχημόνως. Rom 13:13. 1 Thess 4:12.] decently,” that is, to order all our actions and the whole course of our life with good manners.

2\. They are a thing of good report, and that to both parents and children. Now we must do “whatever is of good report.” #footnote[Phil 4:8.]

3\. They work a kind of delight, love and admiration in those who see them, as is noted of the Queen of #footnote[1 Kgs 10:5.] Sheba when she saw the seemly bearing of Solomon’s servants, and of the Egyptians when they saw the orderly #footnote[Gen 43:33.] seating of Joseph’s brothers.

4\. They are an outward ornament to piety and religion, and make it much more respected. In this respect Saint Peter exhorts believing wives to order their conduct well #footnote[1 Pet 3:1.] before unbelieving husbands, and Saint Paul exhorts Christians of every sort to “walk decently toward outsiders.” #footnote[1 Thess 4:12.]

Since the Holy Spirit has urged the point of #emph[good manners] in this way, we must not think it a matter merely for show and a needless point, but a binding duty.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of the objections against good manners.]

1\. #emph[Objection.] Religion and grace do not consist in #emph[good manners.] Many who have not a spark of the fear of God in their hearts are able to conduct themselves very orderly and courteously in their outward behavior.

#emph[Answer.] Though grace does not consist wholly in it, yet grace cannot well be without it: it is a great ornament and beauty to grace. And though good manners may be separated from the fear of God, yet the fear of God will not be separated from good manners. Restraining grace may be in him who has no renewing grace; but renewing grace presupposes restraining grace, just as reason presupposes sense, though sense may be without reason. If those who do not fear God can conduct themselves with seemliness and good manners, what a shame it is for those who seem to fear God not to do so! Will not #emph[those] be a witness against #emph[these?]

2\. #emph[Objection.] Good manners are a hindrance to grace: those who are most diligent in teaching or practicing the one are commonly most negligent in the other.

#emph[Answer.] This is a mere quibble. I am sure that grace is no hindrance to good manners. If any make good manners a hindrance to grace, it is their fault.

3\. #emph[Objection. Good manners] are to #emph[grace] as mint, dill and cumin are to the great and weighty things of the law.

#emph[Answer.] Grant that it is so; yet, seeing both may stand together, why should they be separated? Christ’s rule is this: “These you#super[+] should have practiced, without neglecting the others.” #footnote[Matt 23:23.]

4\. #emph[Objection.] Many who make a great show of religion are very rude and unmannerly.

#emph[Answer.] If there is only a show of religion in them, no wonder they have no manners. If some examples could be shown of those who are truly religious and lack good manners, their pattern is no precedent; much less can it prove that to be no duty which God’s word has set down as a duty. Many who perform some duties well fail much in other duties. Who comes anywhere near following God’s word as he should in everything?

=== §. 30. #emph[Of parents allowing their children to be brought up without manners.]

Contrary to parents’ care in teaching their children good manners is laxness: when parents allow their children to grow up rude, not caring how they behave at home or abroad, toward their parents or toward others. An unmannerly upbringing makes children crooked, perverse, stubborn, churlish, surly and snarling in disposition; as, on the other side, good nurture in this respect breeds a frank and generous spirit, amiability, courtesy and kindness. If those who are brought up without manners are children of those who profess the true religion, they bring a stain on their profession of faith. Indeed, they dishonor God, as if he were the author of unmannerliness and confusion,#footnote[1 Cor 14:33.] against which the Holy Spirit protests. And they make themselves and their children a scorn in the eyes and mouths of profane persons, who will be ready to point and say, “Look at the children of professing Christians, how unmannerly they are brought up, and how badly taught: they do not have so much as good manners in them.”

To avoid this blemish, schoolmasters and all who #footnote[Admonition to schoolmasters.] have the charge of training up young children must help parents in teaching children good manners.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of parents training up their children for some good calling.]

The second branch of #emph[good nurture] is training children up for a good calling. This charge (“Train up a child in #footnote[Prov 22:6.] the way he should go”) aims directly at this. Parents have performed this duty from the beginning of the world, and the Holy Spirit has commended their performance of it. Adam brought up his sons to separate callings: one was “a keeper of sheep,”#footnote[Gen 4:2.] the other “a tiller of the soil.” The like is noted of Jacob’s#footnote[Gen 37:12.] sons, of Laban’s#footnote[Gen 29:9.] and Reuel’s#footnote[Exod 2:16.] daughters, of Jesse’s#footnote[1 Sam 16:11.] son, and of many others.

Much good may arise from this to parents themselves, to their children, and to the people and places where such children #footnote[Benefits of a good calling.] will live.

1\. A good calling is a special means for children to maintain themselves and their family, to relieve those who are in need, to ease their parents, and (if their parents’ need requires it) to relieve and maintain them.

2\. It is that #emph[way] in which God’s angels have a charge to #footnote[Ps 91:11.] keep them while they walk in it.

3\. It is a means in which and by which they may be of service to the commonwealth where they live.

4\. It is the best place in which the general duties of Christianity may be most clearly shown and best performed.

5\. It is the best ordinary means that can be prescribed to keep a child from the vanities of youth, from immoderate pursuit of pleasures, from unlawful games, from idleness, from bad company, and such like evils. As these are sins in themselves, so they are occasions and provocations to other most grievous and monstrous sins, and turn out to be the very bane of youth.

#emph[Objection.] Many parents have good lands to leave to their children: what need is there of a #emph[calling] for such children?

#emph[Answer.] 1. Much land may soon be consumed by those who do not have the skill to use it well.

2\. Maintenance is only one end of a calling, and not the chief and most principal one.

3\. More good may be done by skill in a calling than by a great store of land.

4\. We are born for others as well as for ourselves. It is not enough, therefore, to say, “I have enough to maintain myself.”

=== §. 32. #emph[Of parents’ care in choosing a fit calling for their children.]

Having shown that the point in general is a duty, I will #footnote[Directions for choosing callings. 1. General principles are to be taught.] add some directions for performing it better.

1\. Children are to be trained up in those things which are the groundwork of all callings, such as #emph[reading, writing, and the principles of learning.] Whatever the particular calling is, these will be of great use to anyone. Many who were not taught them at first would give much for them afterward. Parents at first might teach their children these things with much ease and small expense, which afterward cannot be #footnote[#emph[Non sunt contemnenda quasi parua, sine quibus magna constare non possunt.] Hier. #emph[ad Laet.] 2. The calling must be lawful.] so well learned, partly for lack of leisure, and partly because the abilities of those who are grown in years are not as fresh and fit to learn as they were in childhood. Those things are not to be despised as small without which great things cannot stand.

2\. The calling for which children are trained up must be lawful, approved by God’s word, and not against its general rules. So they may keep a good conscience in practicing it.

3\. The calling must be fit for the child who is trained up #footnote[3\. The calling must be fit for the child’s ability.] for it. As there are various callings, so there are various abilities in different children: some are fittest for callings of understanding and learning, others for callings that require an able and strong body. In this the wise, ordering providence of God is much commended, for in this way men are much more useful to one another. Now, in choosing a fit calling, a child’s best #emph[ability,] where it chiefly lies, whether in the exercise of mind or of body, is to be duly observed; and also his inclination, to what calling he is most disposed.

4\. Among fit callings (for there may be many), that which #footnote[4\. The best callings must be preferred. 1 Cor 12:31.] is best and most excellent is to be preferred. To this purpose I may fittingly apply what the apostle says, “eagerly desire the best gifts.”

On this ground let parents be exhorted to train up such #footnote[Children to be trained up for the ministry.] children as they find fit for the great and weighty calling of the ministry. There is no calling in which anyone may do more good, and in which (if they are able and faithful ministers) they can receive more comfort and contentment. This exhortation is all the more to be heeded because, in comparison with those who are trained up for other callings, so few are trained up for this one.

=== §. 33. #emph[Of parents’ faults contrary to their duty of training their children up for a calling.]

On the contrary, many parents offend greatly by not training up their children for a calling as they should. And the offense in this respect is committed in many ways, as

1\. When parents allow their children to live like little masters at home and pass all their youth in idleness. So they turn out mere drones and caterpillars in the commonwealth. If they have an inheritance, they soon waste it; if they have none, they often turn out either thieves or beggars. They will fall in with any company, the readiest prey for the devil that can be; for they are like the house “empty, swept clean, and put in order,”#footnote[Matt 12:44.] and when the evil spirit spies it, he at once enters it with seven other spirits worse than himself.

The wise lawgiver among the heathen is said to have ordered #footnote[Solon legibus cauisse legitur, vt filius, quem parentes nullam artem docuissent, non cogeretur illos alere senio confectos, aut ad inopiam redactos. Eras. Instit. Matrim.] that a child who had been taught no craft by his parents should not be forced to support them, even though they were old or poor. I cite this not to justify the law (for it is against the Christian rule of overcoming evil with good) but to show how the very heathen judged the neglect of this duty a great fault in parents.

2\. When parents neglect to teach their children in their childhood the general and common grounds of all callings, some out of stinginess, others out of carelessness. In this way poor men make their children unfit for many means by which they might well maintain themselves, and rich men make theirs unfit for public office and for good society. Indeed, poor and rich alike are in this way reared all the more in ignorance, and made all the more unfit to profit by the preaching of the word and other means of spiritual edification.

3\. When parents bring up their children in unwarranted and unlawful callings, such as to be of popish and idolatrous orders, to wait on papists, to be stage players, keepers of gambling houses, etc. Some (which is horrible to think of) train up their daughters to be common harlots; and some #footnote[#emph[Phoenices Veneri donum dabant de prostitutione filiarum, antequam iungerent eas viris. Aug de Ciu. D. l.] 4 #emph[c.] 10. \[The Phoenicians gave Venus a gift from the prostitution of their daughters before they joined them to husbands.\] #emph[vide Lud. Viu. in hunc loc. Recte dicuntur parentes filios daemoniorum sacrilegijs obligare conantes, spiritaliter homicidae. Aug. Epist.] 23.] (which is yet more horrible) train up their children to be sorcerers and witches. How can they keep a good conscience in these, when the very works of their calling are sin? Is this not to thrust them headlong into hell? Those who thus bind their children to the devils’ sacrileges are their spiritual murderers.

4\. When parents have no regard at all for the fitness of the calling: as when they train up for learning children of able bodies but dull and slow capacity, with a stuttering tongue and other such imperfections, and in learning they turn out mere dunces and lose all their time, or are not able to make use of the learning they have; or when they train up children who have a great inclination to learning, and are very fit for it, in some other trade, which, after many years spent in it, they are forced to leave.

5\. When parents seek only the most profitable trade, and never think of educating their children most to the honor of God. How can such parents expect God’s blessing on the means used for their children’s good? This is why among papists so many are trained up for church orders and offices, and so few among Protestants. For there is very great maintenance and revenue for such among papists, but little in comparison among Protestants. #footnote[#emph[Infans tibi est? ab infantia sanctitate imbuatur: ab ipsis vnguiculis Spiritui consecretur. Greg. Naz. Orat.] 40. #emph[in S. Bapt. Lege Hier. Epist. ad Laet.] \[Do you have an infant? Let him be steeped in holiness from infancy; let him be consecrated to the Spirit from his tenderest years.\]]

=== §. 34. #emph[Of parents teaching their children piety.]

Parents are to see to the spiritual good of their children, and that in their childhood, as well as to their temporal good. Therefore #emph[parents must train up their children in true piety.] This is expressly commanded in my text under the phrase #emph[admonition of the Lord.] Under the law God both directly #footnote[Deut 4:9; 6:7; 11:19. Ps 78:5, 6.] commanded it, and that very often, and also ordained various outward #footnote[Exod 12:26; 13:14.] rites, and caused many visible and extraordinary #footnote[Josh 4:6, 7, 21.] monuments to be set up, so that children might be moved by them to ask their parents the mystery of them, and parents might take occasion from that question of their children to #footnote[Deut 6:20.] teach them the ordinances of the Lord.

This express charge of the Lord is further commended by the practice which holy parents have given it from time to time: witness the examples of Abraham,#footnote[Gen 18:19.] David,#footnote[Prov 4:4.] Bathsheba,#footnote[Prov 31:1.] Lois#footnote[2 Tim 1:5. #emph[Reasons.] 1. Benefit of piety.] and Eunice, with many others.

1\. #emph[Piety] is the best thing that a parent can teach his child. For as reason makes a man differ from a beast, and as learning and civility make a wise and sober man differ from savages and ruffians, so piety makes a sound Christian differ much more from the most civilized and well-ordered natural man there can be. Learning, civility, calling and portion are all nothing without piety. Now it is a special point of wisdom to seek the best good that we can, for our children as well as for ourselves. Indeed, this is a clear sign of a parent’s true love for his child.

2\. There is a necessity for children to be taught piety, because #footnote[2\. Necessity of piety. #emph[Fiunt non nascuntur Christiani. Hier. ad Laet.]] they are not born but made Christians. By nature they are utterly destitute of all piety; for by nature “every inclination of the thoughts of man’s heart is altogether evil all the time” (Gen 6:5), and in that respect “man is born like a wild donkey’s colt” (Job 11:12). So a man would be better unborn than not taught piety.

3\. The charge and office of parents bind them to teach their #footnote[3\. Charge of parents.] children piety, for they are made watchmen over their children by God. If, therefore, their children live and die in impiety through their parents’ negligence, their blood will be required at their parents’ hands: witness the example of Eli.#footnote[1 Sam 3:13.]

#emph[Objection.] This duty belongs to ministers. #footnote[Why parents as well as ministers are bound to teach piety.]

#emph[Answer.] 1. God has given parents an express charge about this, as we heard before.

2\. In the family a parent is all in all over his child: a king, a priest and a prophet. Therefore what a minister is to do in the matter of instruction in the church, a parent must do at home.

3\. Children need daily instruction. The parents’ pains in this respect must be added to the minister’s, and both together are little enough.

4\. Parents, having more familiarity with their children than ministers have, must surely know their capacity and disposition better, and so may know better in what manner to instruct them: whether to give them milk or solid food, whether to deal mildly or roughly with them.

5\. Instructions from parents are commonly received more kindly than those from others, and children will relish them better when they come from their parents. They say that a plant will grow best in the soil from which it first sprang; so instructions coming from those who both brought us forth and first brought us up are likely to do the most good.

6\. There is no means like this to breed love in a child’s heart: to be an instrument of bringing piety into it. Now who should seek a child’s true love more than a parent? In this respect a parent is more bound to this duty than a minister.

7\. It is impossible that a minister who may have many hundreds of children under his charge should instruct them all well. It is therefore needful that each parent look after his own children.

As these distinct answers satisfy the objection, so they serve as so many more reasons to enforce the duty. Therefore, to proceed in adding other reasons:

4\. It is only right and just that parents teach their children piety, #footnote[4\. Justice.] because they #footnote[Ps 51:5.] conceived them and brought them forth in iniquity. If they allow them to lie and die in that corrupt condition, they are more cruel than the ostrich and the dragon.

5\. Children are most truly God’s: they are born to God.#footnote[Ezek 16:20, 21.] He is #footnote[5\. God’s children.] most truly their parent; in comparison with God, fathers and mothers of the flesh are only nurses. They must therefore nurture them in “the admonition of the Lord.”

6\. Piety is what will make the child, more than any other thing, #footnote[6\. Cause of obedience.] most obedient and grateful to his parents. For so there is a double bond to bind him: 1. His birth. 2. His new birth; especially when the cause of the one has also been the cause of the other.

7\. Nothing can settle the heart of a wise parent on his #footnote[7\. Comfort of parents on their deathbed.] deathbed concerning his children more than the assurance that piety is planted in them. For then he may commend them to God’s providence with stronger confidence, and expect his blessing on them after he is dead with greater assurance.

8\. Parents, by teaching their children piety, are a special #footnote[8\. Propagation of piety.] means of propagating true religion from age to age and from generation to generation. No better means can be thought of. For if all parents were careful of their duty in this respect, then, as there is a succession of children, and by it a preservation of mankind, so there would be a succession of those who fear God, and by it a preservation of true religion. My children who are taught by me may become fathers of children, and so teach their children what they have learned from me. Indeed, after them grandchildren may become fathers of other children and teach their children the same, and so on from age to age, others after them. This is the means on our part: in our time we must do our part, and for those who come after us, leave the outcome to God.

Not only the great benefit of this point but also the all too common neglect of it in most families has moved me to dwell on it the longer and to press it the more forcefully. For performing it better, I will add some directions.

=== §. 35. #emph[Of directing parents how to teach their children true piety.]

1\. Whatever principle of piety parents teach their children, #footnote[1\. Principles of piety all to be taken out of God’s word.] they must be sure that it is grounded on God’s word. This phrase #emph[(admonition of the Lord)] implies as much.

In this way parents will be sure to feed them with good, wholesome spiritual food, such as will serve for their spiritual nourishment and eternal life. In this respect principles of religion grounded on God’s word are called #emph[sound,] or #emph[wholesome,] or #emph[healthful words:] #footnote[2 Tim 1:13 ὑγιαίνοντες λόγοι.] and that both with respect to their matter and substance, and with respect to their effect: they cause and preserve good, sound, spiritual health. We know that natural men take care to give their children such food as is wholesome for their bodies (for will a father #footnote[Matt 7:9, 10, 11.] who is evil give his child a stone or a serpent, and not what is good?). Conscience must move religious parents to have the same care for the souls of their children as nature teaches all parents to have for their bodies. If parents are careful to draw the principles in which they instruct their children out of God’s word, they will be sure not to poison their souls with any error, heresy, superstition or idolatry.

2\. When children begin to read, let them read the holy Scripture. #footnote[2\. Let children at first read the Scripture.] So Timothy was trained up “from infancy” (2 Tim 3:15). In this way children will suck in religion with learning; for there is a secret power lurking in the holy Scripture (which is God’s own word), more than in any books of men, so that through God’s #footnote[#emph[Sacrae Scripturae nominibus vtantur, & loco fabularum selectae illis historiae narrentur, & sententijs prouerbialibus ex Solomone instituantur. Basil. Ascet. c.] 15. 3. Catechize children daily. Deut 6:7.] blessing there may by this means be an inward work of grace in children even in their young years. Besides, no books are easier than many parts of Scripture, and no histories more wonderful and delightful than the histories of Scripture. It is the advice of an ancient father that young children be made acquainted with the words and names of holy Scripture; that, in place of tales and fables, choice histories of the Bible be made known to them; and that they be instructed in the Proverbs of Solomon.

3\. Let children be catechized constantly, from day to day. “You shall teach them continually to your children,” says the law. What is done #emph[daily] is said in Scripture to be done #emph[continually,] as the sacrifice which was offered daily was called #emph[a continual offering.] Here let this #emph[caveat] be noted: in giving this spiritual food, parents should deal with their children as skillful nurses and mothers do in feeding infants. They will not cram more into their mouths at once than their stomachs are able to digest, but will rather feed them often with a little. So it is not fitting for parents to be too tedious: that will only dull a child’s understanding, and breed weariness, and make the child loath to be instructed again. It should rather be “precept on precept, precept on precept, line on line, line on line, #footnote[Isa 28:10.] a little here, a little there.” In this way they will learn with ease and delight; and, this being done often, in time a great measure of knowledge will be gained by it. If a vessel has a little mouth, we do not usually fill it by pouring whole pailfuls on it, for so all may be spilled, and it may receive little or nothing; but we let the liquid fall in little by little, according to the capacity of the mouth. So nothing is lost, and the vessel is filled the sooner. This is how children are to be dealt with.

4\. To the set times of catechizing children let other occasions #footnote[4\. Take every occasion from things of the senses to make a spiritual use.] of teaching them piety be added: as at table, by comparing the spiritual food of their souls to the bodily food by which their bodies are nourished; when they are walking outdoors, by showing them the stars, how they keep steadfast in their course, and the trees, how they bring forth fruit in their season, and how all things are for the use and benefit of man, and so making spiritual uses of them. Note the direction which the law gives parents for this purpose: “You shall talk of My words to your children #footnote[Deut 6:7.] when you sit at home and when you walk along the road, when you lie down and when you get up.”

5\. Let parents explain to their children the mysteries of those #footnote[5\. Explain the mysteries of the rites of the church. Exod 12:26; 13:14.] outward rites which God has ordained in his church, such as #emph[preaching, baptizing children, administering the Lord’s Supper, resting from work on the Lord’s day,] and the like. This was expressly commanded under the law. Visible rites are great helps to the weak understanding of children when they are plainly explained and applied. Indeed, they are also special means of keeping in memory the mysteries contained under them, as coarse threads or wires are means to preserve pearls strung on them. As often as children see the rites, they will be reminded of the mysteries implied under them.

6\. To this may be referred the declaring of such great and #footnote[6\. Let God’s great works be declared.] wonderful works as God has done for his church in former times, especially such works as have been done in their own time; and if any memorials of them remain, make them known to children. This direction was also given under #footnote[Josh 4:6, 21.] the law, concerning the monuments set up for the great deliverances that God gave his people. In particular, when the day of the Gunpowder Treason is observed, parents ought to teach their children the reason for observing that day. And as God does any great works of mercy or of judgment, point them out to children. When there is great famine, plague, or any widespread death, instruct children in the causes of it; when there is victory, plenty, peace or the like, teach children where these come from. Outward things that the senses can perceive work best on children.

7\. Let religious schoolmasters be chosen for children, and so likewise #footnote[7\. Let religious masters be chosen.] other masters to whom children are sent, and religious houses where they are placed. Hannah entrusted her firstborn child to old Eli, a good, religious high priest. #footnote[1 Sam 1:25. #emph[Magister probae aetatis, & vitae, atque eruditionis est eligendus. Hier. ad Laet.] \[A teacher of upright age, life and learning is to be chosen.\]] If masters themselves are religious, there is good hope that they will instruct those under them in piety; and if they do, what a help that will be to parents! If both parents and masters join in it, it must surely be very profitable to the children. If parents should fail, masters might still make up the lack well.

8\. Let parents be a good pattern and example to their children #footnote[8\. Be an example.] in piety. “As for me and my house,” says Joshua, “we will serve the #smallcaps[Lord]”#footnote[Josh 24:15.]: he sets himself first as a guide to the rest. “I will walk in my house with integrity of heart,”#footnote[Ps 101:2. #emph[Nihil in te & in Patre suo videat, quod si fecerit peccet. Mementote vos parentes virginis, magis eam exemplis doceri posse, quàm voce. Hier. ad Laet.] \[Let her see nothing in you or in her father which would be a sin if she did it. Remember, you#super[+] parents of the girl, that she can be taught more by examples than by words.\]] says David, by which he would make himself an example to his children, as to others of his family. Example is a real instruction, and adds a sharp edge to admonition. A religious parent will do much more by practice than by precept. For children are much inclined to follow their parents: let #emph[them] go before, and #emph[children] will soon follow after. Practice is a clear proof of the necessity of the precept given.

=== §. 36. #emph[Of parents’ faults contrary to their duty of teaching their children piety.]

Many are the failings contrary to the care of teaching piety named above. For

1\. Most parents care only for the temporal and worldly good of their children: so long as their children may be well fed and clothed, and brought up in some profitable calling by which they may well maintain themselves in this world, little thought is #footnote[#emph[Job non tantum pro corpore filiorum sollicitus erat, sed plus de anima cogitabat. Orig. in Job. lib.] 1.] given or care taken for their spiritual life in this world or eternal life in the world to come. In what are these parents better than heathen? Job was otherwise minded: he was more concerned for their souls than for their bodies.

2\. Many are so far from teaching piety that they teach their children profaneness, pride, wild living, lying, deceit, and such like principles of the devil. It would have been better for such children to have lived among wild beasts than under such parents. As the children are thrust headlong into hell by this, so their blood will be required of their parents.

3\. Others think it enough that their children are taught a religion, but it does not matter to them what religion. Such are those who, having rich but popish relatives, commit their children to the education of those relatives, in hope of some temporal benefit which their children may reap from them. If they were as careless of their children’s bodies, they would be counted little better than murderers; and is not the soul more precious than the body?

4\. So far are many from catechizing their children, and that daily, that they do not teach them even the Lord’s Prayer, the Creed, and the Ten Commandments. In this the papists will rise up in judgment against them, for they are very diligent in teaching their children #emph[Pater noster, Ave Maria] \[Our Father, Hail Mary\], and such like Latin principles, which the children cannot possibly understand.

5\. Few use the outward helps named above, such as the holy rites appointed by God, the great and glorious works of God, and his extraordinary works of mercy or judgment, to instruct their children by them. As they themselves do not care to take notice of any such thing, so they do not care whether their children do or not.

6\. Some so prefer a little money to the true #footnote[Alexander & in moribus, & in incessu Leonidis paedagogi sui non potuit carere vitijs, quibus adhuc paruulus fuit infectus. Hier. ad Laet. \[Alexander could not rid himself, in his manners and in his gait, of the faults of his tutor Leonides, with which he had been infected while still a little boy.\]] good of their children that they do not care to what schoolmaster they send their children, whether he is profane, or popish, or unlearned, especially if he is a kinsman or one of their friends. Few will do so in the case of their health or outward condition, but will rather get the best physician or the best lawyer they can. Children often learn such evil qualities from their schoolmasters as they can never shake off again.

7\. Many turn out very bad patterns to their children, and set a very bad example by profaneness, dissolute living, swearing, drinking, playing at unlawful games, etc. These parents, as they brought forth their children in sin, so lead them on toward hell. Their evil example is not only a hindrance to the good instruction of others, but also makes all their own counsels (if at any time they do give any good counsel) in vain. For the left hand of evil example #footnote[Parentes non educant filios in eruditione domini quia solliciti non sunt pro salute vel perditione eorum. Orig. loc. citat.] soon pulls down more than the right hand of instruction can build up again.

To conclude: those parents whose children are not brought up in the instruction of the Lord show plainly that they regard neither the salvation nor the damnation of their souls.

=== §. 37. #emph[Of instructing children as soon as they are capable.]

So much for the #emph[kinds] of nurture. Its #emph[time] follows.

In handling the #emph[time] of #footnote[II. The #emph[time] of nurture. Nurture must be begun early.] good nurture, I will show

+ When it ought to be #emph[begun.]
+ How long it ought to be #emph[continued.]

1\. Parents ought to begin to nurture their children as soon as they are capable of any instruction, just as young birds are taught by their mothers to fly as soon as their wings can carry them. #footnote[Prov 22:6.] “Train up #emph[a child],” says Solomon, that is, while he is young and tender; and again, #footnote[Prov 13:24. שחרו מוסר שחר #emph[mane. inde in Piel] שחר #emph[mane facere, id est, mature.] \[שחר: morning; hence, in the Piel, to do in the morning, that is, early.\]] “He who loves his son disciplines him early.” So Samuel was sent when he was very young to be trained up under Eli (1 Sam 1:24); and Solomon was instructed by his father when he was “tender” (Prov 4:3); and Timothy was taught the Scriptures #footnote[2 Tim 3:15 ἀπὸ βρέφους· Βρέφος τὸ ἄρτι γεγονὸς παιδίον. #emph[Puer recens natus.] \[from infancy; brephos is a child just born.\] #emph[Reasons.] 1. Evils prevented.] from a young child, or infant.

There are both #emph[privative] and #emph[positive] reasons to press this point: #emph[privative,] with respect to the harms that may be prevented by it; #emph[positive,] with respect to the good that may be gained by it.

1\. Many are the evils to which children are prone by nature, just as rank ground is apt to bring forth many weeds. For “every inclination of man’s heart is evil from his youth,”#footnote[Gen 8:21.] and “foolishness is bound up in the heart of a child.”#footnote[Prov 22:15.] If, therefore, they are not well nurtured early, what can be looked for but the fruits of #emph[evil] and #emph[folly]? But timely nurture will prevent such fruits, and be an excellent preservative against their own natural corruption, against Satan’s temptations, and against the allurements or discouragements of the world.

2\. Continuing in evil makes children obstinate and #footnote[2\. Continuance in evil makes inflexible. 1 Sam 2:25.] inflexible in it. Eli’s sons, being allowed to go on in wickedness until they came to ripeness of years, would not afterward “listen to their father.” What creature can be tamed if it is not begun with while it is young?

3\. When children first begin to be capable of instruction #footnote[3\. Young children most pliable.] they are most pliable to follow the direction of their parents, as is evident from the ordinary course of nature in all things. Clay, wax and other such things take any impression while they are soft; twigs are bent any way while they are tender; birds and beasts are easily tamed when they are young; grain sown early brings a timely and plentiful harvest.

4\. What is learned in childhood is retained longest: #footnote[4\. What is learned first is remembered best. Prov 22:6. #emph[Quo semel est imbuta recens seruabit odorem Testa diu. Hor.]] “Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it.” A jar keeps longest the scent with which it is first seasoned; and by experience we may note that old men best remember the things which they learned in their childhood. Indeed, by educating children well from their infancy much labor may be spared afterward; for the constant practice of virtue turns it into a law. #footnote[#emph[Si liberis ab ipsis statim initijs leges optimas imponamus, minimum posthac laborabimus. Consuetudo ipsa virtutis in legem transiet. Chrys. in] 1. #emph[Tim. Hom.] 9.]

#emph[Objection.] To teach children while they are young is only like teaching a parrot or some such irrational creature: they may learn what is taught them, but they cannot understand it.

1\. #emph[Answer.] Though children do not have so deep an understanding while they are young as they have when they come to riper years, yet as soon as they are capable of instruction they grasp what is taught them much better than irrational creatures can.

2\. Even if that were granted, it would not follow from it that it is better for them not to be taught while they are young. For, first, it is better that they should be shaped and squared to a good course before they can discern between good and evil than be allowed to run on in evil until they get a habit in it, which, once it is got, will hardly be cast off. Secondly, teaching them before they can well understand helps their understanding much. What can be the reason that the children of kings and great men are commonly of more understanding at 12 or 14 years of age than the children of poor and lowly men at 17 or 18, but that they are instructed sooner and better? Thirdly, as their understanding comes to more and more ripeness with age, they will more and more grasp what at first they did not understand so well. When schoolboys first learn their Latin rules, they learn them merely by rote, without understanding their meaning, #footnote[#emph[Obsequentes facite filios vestros ab infantia, attendentes ne in vos potestatem habeant praeter vestram sententiam. Constit. Apost. l.] 7. #emph[c.] 4. \[Make your#super[+] children obedient from infancy, taking care that they do not get power over you#super[+] against your#super[+] will.\]] but afterward come to make very good use of them. Therefore children are to be instructed early, both for the benefit that may be reaped later, as grain is sown in winter to yield a crop at the following harvest, and to prevent the harms that may come about, so that they do not get the upper hand over their parents against their parents’ will.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of mothers’ distinctive care in nurturing young children.]

By the way, let mothers especially note this point of #emph[timely nurture,] as a point belonging to them in particular. The #footnote[Compare 2 Tim 1:5 with 3:15.] grandmother Lois and the mother Eunice first taught Timothy; Bathsheba#footnote[Prov 31:1.] taught Solomon when he was young. Solomon often warns children #footnote[Prov 1:8; 6:20.] not to forsake #emph[their mother’s teaching.]

By this he implies that mothers should teach their children, especially while they are young. This duty belongs so much to a mother that Solomon#footnote[Prov 29:15.] lays the blame and shame of its neglect on her; on the other side, the honor of nurturing children well goes especially to the mother. It is to this purpose that the #footnote[1 Kgs 15:2, 10.] particular names of the mothers of the kings are recorded in Scripture, suggesting by this that mothers were a main cause of the piety or impiety of such children. Solomon and Absalom both had one father, but different mothers. May we not well think that Solomon’s mother’s care to instruct him was a special means of his piety, and that Absalom’s mother’s neglect of this duty was some cause of his impiety? It is expressly noted of Ahaziah’s mother that she “was his counselor in wickedness,” which made him as wicked #footnote[2 Chr 22:3.] as he was. Experience at home confirms as much: for if father and mother are of different religions, most of the children will follow the mother. For while children are young, their mother is most in their sight: she feeds them, she clothes them, she tends them when they are unwell (when the Shunammite’s child, being ill, said to his father, “My head! My head!” he said to a servant, “Carry him to his mother.”)#footnote[2 Kgs 4:19.] Her precepts and practice are therefore best heeded by the children in that respect, and she has the best opportunity to persuade them to what she likes best. So what they learn in their younger years they commonly learn from their mothers; and what they learn then for the most part sticks closest to them and is retained longest by them, as was noted #footnote[§. 37.] before.

I have not pressed this point on mothers in particular as if I exempted fathers from all care of nurturing their children at the beginning. For in my text the apostle names #emph[fathers;] and Solomon says that his father taught him even while he was tender; and David felt the smart of neglecting his other children. It is therefore (as has been proved before) a joint duty belonging to both. Fathers therefore must do their best, and see that mothers do theirs too, because the father is governor over child, mother and all.

=== §. 39. #emph[Of letting the best time for nurture slip by.]

Contrary to this is the negligence of most parents, who let the best years, in which their children are most teachable, pass by in unruliness, vanity and folly, and so lose what can never be recovered, #emph[most precious time.] This is why, when they set about reclaiming their children, they find them exceedingly perverse and headstrong: much like a strong, big limb of a tree, which, if a man sets about straightening it, he cannot easily make bend; and though it bends, it will not keep standing as he would have it; indeed, it may well break rather than bend much. Such a branch was Adonijah. It is clear that he was not well nurtured in his childhood, for from his childhood “his father had never once reprimanded him.” #footnote[1 Kgs 1:6.]

The father’s putting this duty off on the mother, and the mother’s putting it off on the father, is a great cause of its neglect. If both of them were persuaded that it belonged to them both, and for that reason would help each other as far as they could, children would be much better instructed.

=== §. 40. #emph[Of parents continuing to nurture their children.]

As parents must begin early to nurture their children, so they must keep on with it as long as they retain any government over them; indeed, as long as they remain their parents. There may be some difference in the manner of performing this duty: for children are to be treated as children, and those who are grown in years and of riper understanding are to be dealt with accordingly. Yet parents must always have a care for their well-doing. The word used in my text and translated #emph[children] is a general word which in Scripture and other #footnote[τὰ τέκνα.] writers is used to set forth all sorts of children, of whatever sex, age or rank they are. In handling the duty of children we #footnote[Treatise 5, §. 62.] showed that children owe subjection to their parents as long as they both live. Therefore, by the rule of relation, an authority also remains in parents over their children for as long, and accordingly they ought to have a fatherly care for their good. On this ground Eli did well in admonishing #footnote[1 Sam 2:25. Job 1:5. #emph[Hoc faciebat ille beatus vir, non semel, aut secundo, neque vno anno, aut duobus, sed sic faciebat Iob omnibus diebus vitae eorum. Orig. in Iob lib.] 1. \[This that blessed man did, not once or twice, nor for one year or two; but so Job did all the days of their life.\]] his children after they were married. His fault was that he did not go far enough in doing his duty: he did something, but not all that he should and might have done. Job is commended for the care he had over his children when they were grown in years: for day by day he sent for them and sanctified them, and that after they had separate houses of their own and lived apart; and he did this all their days.

For the performance of what is now urged, parents must so conduct themselves toward their children from time to time that they always keep the reins in their own hands, and #footnote[Direction.] retain a power to curb their children as they see occasion. As their children grow up in years, they may slacken the reins more and more, but never let them go entirely and throw them loose on their children’s necks.

=== §. 41. #emph[Of parents’ folly in letting go all their power over their children.]

Contrary to this is the folly of those who put themselves in their children’s power and let go all their authority over them. Many #footnote[Aegrè reprendas quod sinis consuescere. Hier. ad Laet. \[You will hardly correct what you allow to become a habit.\]] parents who have done this, having found by woeful experience the injury and harm that followed from it, have much repented their folly and used means of redress, but all too late. For an injury is much more easily prevented than redressed. All the power that David had could not hold Absalom in after he was permitted to have #footnote[2 Sam 15:1.] horses and chariots and men at his command. If David had continued as he began, to keep him within bounds and always hold him under, all the treasonous plots which Absalom put into action might easily have been prevented. Our times afford too many examples of parents’ folly in this respect, and of the injuries following from it.

So much for the time of nurture. Its means follow.
